<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=morse-code">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morse code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as American Morse code, International Morse code</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morse code is a method of telecommunication that encodes letters, numerals and punctuation as standard sequences of two signal durations, dots and dashes. Around 1837 the American artist Samuel Morse devised a code of numerals for the electrical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> he was developing with Joseph Henry and Alfred Vail; in 1840 Vail extended it to letters, giving the shortest signs to the commonest ones, and this code was first used in 1844. Friedrich Gerke's simpler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> alphabet of 1848, adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1851, led to the International Morse code of 1865, which also carried early radio communication from the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morse-code.jpg" alt="Morse code" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hp.Baumeler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morsetaste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1837</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Samuel Morse · Alfred Vail</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The alphabet that carries Morse's name was Alfred Vail's, who extended Morse's numerals-only design in 1840, and most of the present international code comes from Friedrich Gerke's revision of 1848.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Electrical telegraph<span>1816</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=morse-code">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
